<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarcophagus of the Spouses</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 520 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sarcophagus of the Spouses is an Etruscan terracotta funerary urn from Caere, made around 520 BCE, showing a married couple reclining together at a banquet. Found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banditaccia-necropolis">Banditaccia necropolis</a> in 1881 and now in the National Etruscan Museum of the Villa Giulia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, it is celebrated for the warmth of its figures and as evidence of the standing of Etruscan women. A similar sarcophagus is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarcophagus-of-the-spouses.jpg" alt="Sarcophagus of the Spouses" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarcophagus_of_the_Spouses_from_Villa_Giulia_in_Rome.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
